# Workspace Index

AI-assisted development workspace for **{{.ProjectName}}**.

## Top-level map

| Folder | Lifecycle | What lives here |
|---|---|---|
| `lab/` | Experimental. Often throwaway. | Executable scripts to probe APIs, capture flow, reproduce bugs. |
| `adr/` | Durable. Append-only. | Lightweight ADRs for one-way doors. |
| `agents/` | Durable. Config. | Where engineering skills find the issue tracker, triage labels, and domain docs. |
| `GLOSSARY.md` | Durable. Canonical. | Domain vocabulary. Created lazily by `/domain-modeling`. |
| `out-of-scope/` | Durable. | One file per rejected concept (`wontfix`). Created lazily by `/triage`. |

In-flight work (tickets, draft glossary terms, draft ADRs) lives copy-local under `<copy-root>/.scratch/`, not here. See `agents/README.md`.

## `lab/` -- the experimental layer

Each experiment is a folder, not a loose script:

```
lab/<vendor>/<experiment>/
  README.md      what this experiment proved (or didn't)
  script.py      latest version -- avoid v2/v3/v4 sprawl
  fixtures/
  runs/          captured outputs (rotate aggressively)
```

`lab/_scratch/` is for one-off pokes. Prune often.

## Conventions

- Lowercase, kebab-case paths.
- Every directory has a `README.md` (one paragraph: what's here, when to add to it).
- Time-bound files use ISO date prefixes (`YYYY-MM-DD-...`).
- Folders prefixed with `_` (e.g. `_scratch`) are meta and sort to the bottom.
- Frontmatter on docs where useful: `status`, `domain`, `owner`, `updated`, `related-pr`.
